<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Using Inequalities to Solve a Problem</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00543</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Using Inequalities to Solve a Problem</md:title>
    <md:uuid>89998b50-7119-4a92-9c28-086a05a2dfab</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001">      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Use the following information to answer questions 1 – 2.</para>
      <quote id="quote-00001">Han is about to mow some lawns in his neighborhood. His lawn mower has a 5-gallon
        fuel tank, but Han is not sure how much gasoline is in the tank.</quote>
        <quote id="quote-00002">He knows,
        however, that the lawn mower uses 0.4 gallon of gasoline per hour of mowing.</quote>
        <quote id="quote-00003">
          
            <media alt="A lawnmower on a lawn.">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_12_03_01.jpg"/>
            </media>
          
        </quote>

      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->

      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">What are all the possible values for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, the number of hours Han can mow without
            refilling the lawn mower?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00005"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mspace width="0.278em"/><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mspace width="0.278em"/><m:mn>12.5</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mspace width="0.278em"/></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mspace width="0.278em"/><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mspace width="0.278em"/><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Write one or more inequalities to represent your response. Be prepared to show your
            reasoning.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00008"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mn>0.4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>12.5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. If the tank is completely
            full, Han can mow for at most 12.5 hours without refilling. If it is all the way empty,
            he can mow for 0 hours. It is not possible for Han to mow for a negative number of
            hours.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Video: Using Inequalities to Solve a Problem</title>
      <para id="para-00009">Watch the following video to learn more about how to solve a problem using inequalities.</para>
      <para id="para-00002"><link url="https://openstax.org/r/inequalities-problem">Using Inequalities to Solve a Problem</link></para>
    </section>

    </section>
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
        <exercise id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00010"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_2_10_2_SC"/></para>
          </problem>
          </exercise>
        </note>
    </section>

    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00005">
        <title>Using Inequalities to Solve a Problem</title>
        <para id="para-00011">Let’s look at an example where we might be able to solve a problem using inequalities.</para>
        <para id="para-00012">A school is repainting its classroom walls. The school has at most 30 gallons of
          paint. The painter needs <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> gallons of paint for each classroom. How many
          classrooms can the school paint at most?</para>
        <para id="para-00013">We know that the answer is greater than or equal to 0 classrooms, so <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        <para id="para-00014">We also know that each classroom requires <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> or 2.5 gallons of paint.</para>
        <para id="para-00015">Let’s take a guess at how many classrooms and check if we are close to the solution.
          How many gallons of paint would we need to paint 10 classrooms?</para>
        <para id="para-00016"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo>×</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>25</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, and since <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>25</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, it could be possible for the school to
          paint 10 classrooms.</para>
        <para id="para-00017">We can try 11 classrooms.</para>
        <para id="para-00018"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>11</m:mn><m:mo>×</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>27.5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, and since <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>27.5</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, it could be possible for the school
          to paint 11 classrooms.</para>
        <para id="para-00019">We can try 12 classrooms.</para>
        <para id="para-00020"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>12</m:mn><m:mo>×</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, and since <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, the most classrooms the school could paint
          is 12 classrooms.</para>

        <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00006"><note class="try" id="note-00002">
        <title>Using Inequalities to Solve a Problem</title>
        <para id="para-00021">Use the following information for questions 1 – 2.</para>
        <quote id="quote-00004">While working in a car factory, a worker can use a drill for as long as the
          batteries last. The worker has at most 9 fully charged batteries. Each car the worker
          helps assemble uses <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> of a full battery.</quote>
<!--Text
        Entry Interaction Start -->
        <exercise id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00023"> What are all of the possible values for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, the number of cars the work can
              help assemble? Write one or more inequalities to represent your response.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00025">Compare your answer: </para>

            <para id="para-00026"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>12</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--Interaction
        End -->
        <!--Text
        Entry Interaction Start -->
        <exercise id="exer-00004">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00027"> Explain the meaning of the inequalities.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00029">Compare your answer: </para>

            <para id="para-00030">If the worker has 9 fully charged batteries, they can help assemble up to 12 cars.
              If the worker has no batteries, they can assemble 0 cars. It is not possible for the
              worker to assemble less than 0 cars.</para>

          </solution>
        </exercise>
        </note></section>
        <!--Interaction
        End -->
      </section>

    </section>
  </content>
